ght:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span>. So <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span></span></span></span> transduces\nall graphs. Conversely, a transduction of all graphs produces the\nincidence graph between <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">{0, …, m-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\">…</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span> and its subsets, so its edge\nformula has the independence property.\n\nThe argument is carried out for the graph classes of the archive and\nmathlib formulas, and transferred to the graph classes used\nhere and the translation\nof formulas."},"p:Lax185574Proofs.IndependencePropertyTuples.not_hasIP_of_monadicallyDependent":{"href":"../lax-185574/Lax185574Proofs.IndependencePropertyTuples.not_hasIP_of_monadicallyDependent.html","incident":["e:Lax185574Proofs.IndependencePropertyTuples.not_hasIP_of_monadicallyDependent:conclusion:Lax185574.IndependenceProperty.not_hasIP_of_monadicallyDependent:0"],"kind":"proof","label":"Suppose `φ(x̄; ȳ)` has the independence property on `C`. The left tuple is\nnot empty, since otherwise all `aᵢ` agree. By Shelah's reduction, carried\nout with Ramsey's theorem on finite shattered sequences, some formula\n`ψ(x; z̄)` with a single left variable has the independence property, and\nso has its dual `ψ*(z̄; x)`. Then `ψ*(z̄; x) ∧ ψ*(z̄'; x)` codes pairs: for\ntuples `aᵢ` and `a_{n+j}` the elements `b_{{k, n+l}}` satisfy it exactly\nwhen `(i, j) = (k, l)`. Among all formulas coding on `C`, with any number\nof colors, take one of least total arity. If it has two left variables,\nthen either `x` extends by elements of a color `A` to a tuple coding `z`\nand no other element of a color `C`, which is a coding formula with a\nsingle left variable, or a spurious witness gives a coding formula with\nparameters and fewer left variables. By symmetry the minimal formula is\n`θ(x, y, z)` in single variables. With a color `C₀` for the codes `c_{i,J}`\nwith `i ∈ J`, the formula `∃z (C₀(z) ∧ θ(x, y, z))` has the independence\nproperty. So `C` is not monadically dependent.\n\nThe argument is carried out for the graph classes of the archive and\nmathlib formulas, and transferred to the graph classes used\nhere and the translation\nof formulas.","nodeId":"p:Lax185574Proofs.IndependencePropertyTuples.not_hasIP_of_monadicallyDependent","semanticId":"Lax185574Proofs.IndependencePropertyTuples.not_hasIP_of_monadicallyDependent","tooltipHtml":"Suppose <code>φ(x̄; ȳ)</code> has the independence property on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex